WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.701
[music playing]

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.703
NARRATOR: The sub will
dive to the wreck site,

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.407
travel over the bow, then
out across the debris field,

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.342
searching for the
mysterious piece of metal.

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:15.081
Here comes the water attempt.

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.116
Tom, are you ready?

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.485
Yeah, roger that,
my hatch is secure.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.920
Roger that, hatch
secure, here we go.

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:21.988
Roger that.

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:23.223
If we could get a little
bit more on there.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.492
NARRATOR: If Parks's
theory is correct,

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.161
it'll be an incredible
discovery, connecting

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:31.030
a family across generations.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:34.267
Cleared to go ahead
and start pumping.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:37.637
There goes the submerge.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.606
[radio chatter]

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.841
This is the LF, go ahead?

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:44.144
You're cleared
to start pumping.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.278
[radio chatter]

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.146
It's clear.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.881
I have armed thrusters.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.315
We are beginning to pump in now.

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:04.964
NARRATOR: The sub
begins its long descent.

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:11.504
Benjamin Guggenheim was
born into a wealthy mining

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:17.110
family in Philadelphia in 1865.

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:21.581
He was 47 years old when Titanic
sank, leaving behind a wife

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.149
and three young daughters.

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:33.860
Surface LF, depth
3805, heading 310.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.295
Life support good.

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:37.297
Wreck on sonar.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:38.898
Roger that, LF.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:44.370
Understand 3805 meters heading
310 degrees, with life support

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:45.805
good and wreck on sonar.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:46.940
Congratulations.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:51.110
NARRATOR: The sub has
reached the wreck site.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:52.979
PARKS STEPHENSON:
God, it's murky.

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:54.747
VICTOR VESCOVO: You can look
down there and see for--

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:55.782
[interposing voices]

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.251
PARKS STEPHENSON:
I can see it, yeah.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:00.587
Actually, the visibility
is better looking down.

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:01.688
(LAUGHING) Yes it is.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.122
There's the Marconi
room, one of the windows,

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.090
one of the skylights.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.492
VICTOR VESCOVO:
Oh, let's see here.

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:08.228
NARRATOR: Before searching for
the remains of the Guggenheim

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.364
state rooms, the team
films additional footage

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:14.868
of other important features
for later analysis,

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:18.071
including the area around
the captain's quarters.

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:21.274
OK, I can barely see it.

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:23.843
Barely see it, and
yeah, it's devastated.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.745
I see a glimpse of the tub.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.689
NARRATOR: Next, the team turns
their attention to the search

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:41.027
for Guggenheim's staterooms.

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:45.331
The debris field is 15
square miles in size.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:47.667
Finding a single piece
of metal is like trying

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:52.038
to find a needle in a haystack.

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.773
Is this the bigger piece?

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.042
No, it's not the bigger piece.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:57.377
What is this?

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.645
PARKS STEPHENSON: I don't
think it's Guggenheim either.

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:06.085
NARRATOR: Parks is searching
for specific structural features

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.654
that coincide with
the stateroom's

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.989
location aboard the ship.

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:13.226
This is key to confirming
the identity of the mystery

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.227
object in the image.

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:20.733
We've got something
up there on the left.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.935
Yep.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:22.869
40 meters.

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:28.474
Oh, whoa, whoa, whoa, eyes
out, eyes out, eyes out.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:29.242
OK.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:36.015
PARKS STEPHENSON: This might
be the Guggenheim piece.

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.383
You see all that ribbing?

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.151
Yeah.

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.122
This might be the
Guggenheim piece.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.123
I'm going to translate left.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.592
You translate
left, yes, please.

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.098
Yep, here we go, that is it.

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.601
This is it, this is Guggenheim's
stateroom right here.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:58.705
Right here.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:03:59.505
VICTOR VESCOVO: Found it.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.577
That's the
stateroom right there.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:08.247
That's Guggenheim.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.315
Good job.

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.083
Teamwork.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:17.724
Surface, LF, present
depth 3802, life support

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.692
good, at Guggenheim.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.894
Roger, LF, at Guggenheim.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:22.695
Congratulations.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.131
[music playing]

